= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Write $h_A=\mathcal C(A,-)$. The covariant <Yoneda lemma> gives the natural bijection
$$
\operatorname{Nat}(h_A,F)\cong F(A),\qquad\alpha\longmapsto\alpha_A(1_A),
$$
whose inverse sends $x\in F(A)$ to the <natural transformation> with component $u:A\to B\mapsto F(u)(x)$. This also identifies the two variables' naturality.

To prove that $h_A$ is a <projective object in a category>, let $p:G\twoheadrightarrow F$ be an <epimorphism> in the <functor category> and $\alpha:h_A\to F$. By <pointwise epimorphism in a functor category>, lift $\alpha_A(1_A)$ through the <surjective function> $p_A$. The <Yoneda lemma> turns this lift into $\beta:h_A\to G$ with $p\beta=\alpha$.

For a <small category>, the set of pairs $(A,x)$ with $x\in F(A)$ indexes the canonical map
$$
p:\coprod_{(A,x)}h_A\longrightarrow F,\qquad
p_B(A,x,u)=F(u)(x).
$$
It is natural, and every $y\in F(B)$ is the image of $(B,y,1_B)$. Thus it is an <epimorphism>, establishing the <projective cover of a set-valued functor by representables> construction. Here cover means a projective presentation; no minimality is asserted.

For the equivalence's first step, if $f:B\to D$ is a <monomorphism>, then $h_A(f)$, the map $u\mapsto fu$, is injective for every $A$. Hence every <representable functor> is a <monofunctor>. Conversely, injectivity of every $h_A(f)$ is exactly left cancellation against every $u,v:A\to B$, so it implies that $f$ is a <monomorphism>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{(a)}\Longleftrightarrow\text{(b)},\qquad
\coprod_{(A,x)}h_A\twoheadrightarrow F.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Assume (b). A <coproduct in a category> of <monofunctors> in $[\mathcal C,\mathbf{Set}]$ is again a <monofunctor>: at an object it is the disjoint union of the values, and every arrow acts within the same summand by an <injective function>. Distinct summands cannot become identified, and injectivity within a summand follows from its <monofunctor> property.

Apply this to the coproduct of <representable functors> constructed in part (a). Its canonical map to $F$ is a <pointwise epimorphism in a functor category>. Thus every $F$ is an epimorphic image of a <monofunctor>, proving
$$
\boxed{\text{(b)}\Longrightarrow\text{(c)}.}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Assume (c), and take an <epimorphism> $p:M\twoheadrightarrow h_A$ with $M$ a <monofunctor>. Since $h_A$ is a <projective object in a category>, there is a <natural transformation> $s:h_A\to M$ with $ps=1_{h_A}$. A <retract in a category> of a <monofunctor> is a <monofunctor>: if $h_A(f)(u)=h_A(f)(v)$, then naturality of $s$ gives $M(f)s(u)=M(f)s(v)$, injectivity of $M(f)$ gives $s(u)=s(v)$, and applying $p$ gives $u=v$. Thus (c) implies (b), completing
$$
\boxed{\text{(a)}\Longleftrightarrow\text{(b)}\Longleftrightarrow\text{(c)}.}
$$

The stronger assertion that every set-valued <functor> is a <monofunctor> holds exactly when $\mathcal C$ is a <groupoid>. Sufficiency follows because a <functor> preserves inverses, so sends every arrow to a bijection.

For necessity, fix $f:A\to B$, and form the pointwise <pushout in a category>
$$
P=h_A\amalg_{h_B}h_A,
$$
using twice the <natural transformation> $h_B\to h_A$ given by precomposition with $f$. At an object $X$, $P(X)$ consists of two copies of $\mathcal C(A,X)$, with the two copies of $kf$ identified for every $k:B\to X$. No different underlying elements are identified: each generating relation simply joins the two copies of one element. In $P(B)$, the two copies of $f$ coincide, since $f=1_Bf$. Hence $P(f)$ sends the two copies of $1_A$ in $P(A)$ to the same element. If $P$ is a <monofunctor>, those copies of $1_A$ coincide. The description of the pushout implies $rf=1_A$ for some $r:B\to A$. Thus every morphism is a <split monomorphism>.

Apply the same conclusion to $r$: there is $t:A\to B$ with $tr=1_B$. Then $t=trf=f$, and so $fr=1_B$ as well. Every arrow is invertible, proving
$$
\boxed{\text{Every }F:\mathcal C\to\mathbf{Set}\text{ is a monofunctor}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \mathcal C\text{ is a groupoid}.}
$$

